[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Gameplay Ability System (GAS) Architecture and Implementation in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Gameplay Ability System (GAS) is a highly flexible, robust framework provided by Epic Games in Unreal Engine designed for building RPG-style abilities, status effects, and attributes. This tutorial covers the foundational architecture of GAS and provides a step-by-step implementation guide."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of GAS"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ability System Component (ASC): The heart of GAS. It acts as the bridge between the actor and the system, holding attributes, abilities, and active gameplay effects."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Gameplay Attributes: Numeric values representing stats like Health, Mana, and Stamina, managed via Attribute Sets."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Gameplay Abilities (GA): The logic and behavior of what an action does (e.g., casting a fireball, executing a melee swing)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Gameplay Effects (GE): Modifiers applied to attributes, either instantly, over time (buffs/debuffs), or infinitely."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Setting up the Character and ASC"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use GAS, your character must implement the "Ability System Interface" and instantiate an Ability System Component within its class defaults."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"UCLASS()
class AMyCharacter : public ACharacter, public IAbilitySystemInterface
{
    GENERATED_BODY()
public:
    virtual UAbilitySystemComponent* GetAbilitySystemComponent() const override;

protected:
    UPROPERTY(VisibleAnywhere, BlueprintReadOnly, Category = "Abilities")
    class UAbilitySystemComponent* AbilitySystemComponent;
};"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Creating an Attribute Set"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Define your custom attributes by inheriting from "UAttributeSet". Use the macro "GAMEPLAYATTRIBUTE_PROPERTY_GETTER" to simplify attribute replication and modification."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"UCLASS()
class UMyAttributeSet : public UAttributeSet
{
    GENERATED_BODY()
public:
    UPROPERTY(BlueprintReadOnly, Category = "Attributes", ReplicatedUsing = OnRep_Health)
    FGameplayAttributeData Health;
    ATTRIBUTE_ACCESSORS(UMyAttributeSet, Health);

    UFUNCTION()
    void OnRep_Health(const FGameplayAttributeData& OldHealth);
};"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Implementing a Gameplay Ability"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new blueprint or C++ class derived from "UGameplayAbility". Override the "ActivateAbility" function to define the ability's payload."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"void UMyGameplayAbility::ActivateAbility(const FGameplayAbilitySpecHandle Handle, const FGameplayAbilityActorInfo* ActorInfo, const FGameplayAbilityActivationInfo ActivationInfo, const FGameplayEventData* TriggerEventData)
{
    Super::ActivateAbility(Handle, ActorInfo, ActivationInfo, TriggerEventData);

    // Insert custom ability logic here

    EndAbility(Handle, ActorInfo, ActivationInfo, true, false);
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"By structuring your project around GAS, you ensure seamless multiplayer replication, scalable stat management, and decoupled gameplay logic. You can now build complex combinations of Gameplay Effects and Tags to drive your game combat systems."}]
